And there, in the central part, surrounded by a host of maidens numbering many thousands, seated below in a circle arranged around the perimeter, with masses of glittering ornaments, like a cluster of wish-fulfilling creepers — leaning against a not-too-large couch covered with a blue silk covering-cloth, resting with her doubled vine-like arms placed upon a white cushion — like the earth hanging from the tusk of the Great Boar (<s>Mahāvarāha</s>); being fanned by chowrie-bearers who seemed to swim through the expansive water-net of her body's radiance by the circling motions of their vine-like arms; as if being carried away by <s>Nāgas</s> on the gem pavements below through her fallen reflections; as if being separately led away by the guardians of the quarters on the gem walls nearby; as if being lifted up by the gods in the gem canopies above; as if taken into the heart by the great gem pillars; as if drunk in by the palace mirrors; as if being made to ascend to the vault of the sky by the world of <s>Vidyādharas</s> scattered across the ornate underside of the splendid canopy, facing downward; surrounded as if by the three worlds gathered together in a mass of curiosity to behold her, under the pretext of paintings; being gazed upon even by the palace itself, which had, as it were, produced thousands of eyes out of curiosity, in the form of the colorful eye-spots of hundreds of peacocks dancing to the sound of ornaments; being examined even by her own attendants with unblinking eyes, as if they had acquired divine sight from the desire to see her; her every limb occupied by the auspicious marks as if possessed by passion; relinquishing childhood as if it were something of no merit; though not given in marriage, being seized, as it were, by youth that was helplessly overcome by the possession of <s>Manmatha</s> (the God of Love);
With her two feet setting forth, as it were, a river of coral juice — endowed with toes bearing circles of nail-gems like constellations on the surface of the earth, with fingers that seemed to pour forth a rain of blood-streams through the nail-gaps with a gentle rhythm, causing doubt whether they were lines of red rays from her foot-ornaments, as if hanging from the thread-tips of her worn red garment, as if flowing down as braids of beauty-water reddened by lac-juice, as if emanating from the undisturbed rays of the lac-dye on her feet;
Her hip region being touched by the circle of rays from her anklet-gems rising upward, as if to aid the pair of thighs wearied by the heavy burden of her hips; bearing a pair of thighs that had become two like an exudation of loveliness, having flowed from the firmly pressed middle part by <s>Prajāpati's</s> hand and from the impact against the rock-surface of her hips; with a halo created for the orb of her hips by the girdle-chain — its circle of long rays extending in all directions, as if blocking the sight of other men out of jealousy, as if spreading out in breadth from curiosity, as if releasing horripilation from the pleasure of touch; with hips exceedingly heavy, as if from the burden of the hearts of all people falling upon them; with her waist wasting away, as if from the grief of not being able to see her face hidden behind her elevated breasts;
Bearing a whirl-like navel circle that seemed to have sunk in from the imprint of <s>Prajāpati's</s> fingers due to her extreme delicacy when he touched her; bearing a line of fine hair (<s>romarāji</s>) like a row of letters of a victory-inscription over the three worlds inscribed by <s>Manmatha</s>; adorned with the weight of breasts that seemed to be the footstool of <s>Makaraketu</s> (the God of Love), thrust forth as if by the palms of a heart oppressed by excessive weight, with the reflection of ear-ornament-sprouts having entered within; resplendent with arms like lotus-stalks of pure beauty-water, as if extending from the downward-facing rays of her ear-ornaments; illuminated by a pair of hands that seemed to release a network of streams of perspiration-water from the exertion of bearing the weight of ruby bracelets, raining clusters of rays from their nails;
Her chin region held up by high hands, as if by a necklace raising a face bowed down by the weight of breasts; with two lips red as coral creepers, as if risen as waves from an ocean of passion stirred by the wind of fresh new youth; with a pair of cheeks of red-white-clear luster, having the appearance of a casket of ruby shells filled with wine-juice; radiant with a nose-bridge beautiful as the jeweled corner of <s>Rati's</s> <s>vīṇā</s>;
With the outer corners of her eyes slightly reddened, as if from the anger of the ears at being obstructed in the spread of their movement; with a pair of eyes that were a milk-ocean serving as the abode of the <s>Lakṣmī</s> of her own face — intent on making the entire world of the living seem to consist of nothing but eyes;
With her forehead-surface illuminated by a <s>tilaka</s> dot affixed to her face, like the heart of <s>Manmatha</s> painted with <s>manaḥśilā</s> (realgar) paste and possessed by passion, between the two rut-lines, as it were, of the elephant of intoxicating youth; wearing an ear-ornament causing doubt whether it was streams of honey dripping from ear-lotuses set free, made of fine gold plate, bearing swinging leaf-patterned <s>makara</s>-shaped ruby earrings; her long mass of hair being washed, as it were, with wine-juice by the streaming web of rays from the crest-jewel that kissed her hair-parting and made her forehead rosy;
As if seeking to surpass <s>Gaurī</s>, who was proud of having entered half the body of <s>Hara</s> (<s>Śiva</s>), displaying through every limb a special beauty shown by <s>Manmatha</s> who had entered her entire body; creating, as it were, hundreds of <s>Lakṣmīs</s> from her own form through her reflections, to remove the pride of <s>Nārāyaṇa</s> who was delighted by the one <s>Lakṣmī</s> placed upon his chest; scattering, as it were, thousands of moons in all directions through her coquettish smiles, to destroy the pride of <s>Hara</s> who was astonished at having one moon placed upon his head; producing myriads of <s>Manmathas</s> in every heart, as if in anger at the Lord of destruction who mercilessly burned the one <s>Manmatha</s>;
Making little sand-banks with lotus-pollen sand in the pleasure-streams for the pet <s>cakravāka</s> couple, weary from their night-vigil, to sleep upon; commanding the goose-keeper, 'Bind the beloved swan-couple, which has set out startled by the sound of the attendants, with a lotus-fiber fetter and bring them back!'; giving sprouts of barley, taken from a friend's ear, to a palace fawn that was licking the emerald rays of her ornaments; honoring with the gift of all her ornaments the garden-keeper who had come to announce the first blooming of a creeper she herself had raised; again and again engaging in conversation the <s>Śabarī</s> woman from the pleasure-hill, who had brought leaf-cups filled with various forest flowers and fruits, whose speech was unintelligible, for the sake of amusement; playing with bees that were like blue balls, blinded by the fragrance of her face, repeatedly struck by her palms and repeatedly flying up; laughing and tapping on the head with a play-lotus the chowrie-bearer who had given a mischievous smile upon hearing the speech of the caged <s>hārīta</s> bird; striking the betel-box carrier on the breast with a fist of cloth, with what seemed to be the intention of leaving nail-marks adorned with a network of perspiration-drops from a reflection transferred to a pearl-studded crescent-ornament; laughing at the chowrie-bearer, suspecting that the dense reflections of ruby earrings on her cheeks were circles of fresh nail-marks, and covering them on her cheek with her own ear-lotus sprout given under the pretext of a gracious gift;
Like the earth, seated upon the coils of <s>Śeṣa</s> after the great noble mountains had been removed; like the beauty of the spring month, with feet dusty-grey from flower-pollen carried away by swarms of bees; like autumn, its peacock-pride taken away by the cries of birds born in Lake <s>Mānasa</s>; like <s>Gaurī</s>, with a head-ornament fashioned of white garments; like the line of seaside forests, with groves of <s>tamāla</s> trees dark with swarms of bees; like the moon, her weighty consort seized by the unbridled play of <s>Manmatha</s>; like a forest line, her middle adorned with pale-dark clinging creepers; like the beauty of dawn, adorned with the radiance of shining pearls split by rubies; like a lotus-pond of the sky, with the delicate root of her thighs visible through her clear garment like lotus-stalks; like a row of peacocks, with the lustrous beauty of <s>candrakānta</s> (moon-gems) shimmering from the mass of tail-feathers kissing the hips; like a wish-fulfilling creeper, granting the fruits of desire —
Thus he beheld <s>Kādambarī</s>, who, with <s>Keyūraka</s> seated before her near the bed, was questioning him again and again in conversation connected only with <s>Candrāpīḍa</s> — 'Who is he? Whose offspring is he? What is his name? What is his appearance like? How old is he? What does he say? What did you tell him? How long did you see him? How did his acquaintance with <s>Mahāśvetā</s> arise? Will he come here?' — while <s>Keyūraka</s> was eloquent in describing his form.
